>
<section class="page-head" style="--ph-img: url(/style/img/izrada/akvarijum.webp); --ph-img-sm: url(/style/img/izrada/akvarijum.webp)">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h1 set:html={twoTone('Red Moor Wood')} />
        <p>
          Natural root-like decoration for aquascapes and biotope aquariums —
          a branching structure, long life and beneficial tannins that enrich
          the aquatic ecosystem.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
<div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb reveal">
      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol>
          <li><a href="/en/">Home</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en/aquariums.html">Guides</a></li>
          <li>Red Moor Wood</li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </div></div>
<section class="guide-body">
      <div class="wrap">

        <!-- Sta je Red Moor Wood -->
        <div class="guide-section reveal">
          <h2>What is Red Moor Wood?</h2>
          <p>
            Red Moor Wood is a type of root wood that comes from the marshlands
            of northern and central Europe. It is recognizable by its heavily
            branched, root-like structure and reddish-brown color, which
            darkens over time in water. Every piece is unique in shape and
            size, which makes it a favorite material among aquascapers who aim
            for a natural, organic look in their aquariums.
          </p>
          <p>
            Unlike some other types of wood used in fishkeeping, Red Moor Wood
            is very durable and does not rot in water. Thanks to its dense
            structure, it keeps its shape for years and looks better and
            better as it develops a natural patina. It is ideal for creating a
            natural look in aquariums and paludariums.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Prednosti -->
        <div class="guide-section reveal">
          <h2>Benefits in the aquarium</h2>
          <div class="guide-card">
            <h3>Aquascaping and biotope design</h3>
            <p>
              The branching structure of Red Moor Wood is perfect for creating
              natural biotope layouts — swamp forests, root-filled rivers and
              the blackwaters of the Amazon or Southeast Asia. It is used as the
              centerpiece of a composition or as a visual frame for mosses and
              plants.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card">
            <h3>Habitat for fish and invertebrates</h3>
            <p>
              The branching arms and hiding spots attract many fish species —
              especially smaller characins, corydoras and shrimp. Corydoras and
              otocinclus actively search the wood for algae and the biofilm
              that naturally forms on its surface.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card">
            <h3>A base for mosses and plants</h3>
            <p>
              Red Moor Wood is an excellent base for attaching mosses and plants
              such as Anubias, Java Fern (Microsorum pteropus) and
              Bucephalandra. The roots easily grip the rough surface of the
              wood without any glue — just tie them on with thread and wait a
              few weeks.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="guide-card">
            <h3>Durability</h3>
            <p>
              Unlike soft woods that break down quickly, Red Moor Wood is
              extremely durable. Water does not affect its structure — the wood
              stays solid and attractive for years.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Priprema -->
        <div class="guide-section reveal">
          <h2>How to prepare Red Moor Wood for an aquarium</h2>
          <p>
            Before placing the wood in your aquarium, we recommend the
            following steps to avoid cloudy water and make sure the wood
            sinks.
          </p>

          <h3>1. Rinsing</h3>
          <p>
            Rinse the wood thoroughly under cold running water to remove dust,
            sand and fine particles. Use a brush for larger pieces.
          </p>

          <h3>2. Soaking (1–2 weeks)</h3>
          <p>
            Submerge the wood in a bucket or container of water and leave it
            for 1 to 2 weeks. During this time the wood will release tannins
            that tint the water yellow or brown. Change the water every 2–3
            days until it is clear enough. This is also the only way for the
            wood to become waterlogged and sink without support.
          </p>

          <h3>3. Boiling (optional)</h3>
          <p>
            Briefly boiling the wood (20–30 minutes) speeds up tannin release
            and sterilizes it. It is recommended for smaller pieces or when
            you want faster results. After boiling, let the wood cool down
            before placing it in the aquarium.
          </p>

          <h3>4. Securing it in the aquarium</h3>
          <p>
            If the wood still floats after soaking, temporarily weigh it down
            with a stone or use an aquarium strip as a support. The wood will
            sink completely once it is waterlogged enough — usually after 2 to
            4 weeks in water.
          </p>

          <div class="tip-box">
            <p>
              <strong>Tip:</strong> The tannins the wood releases are not
              harmful — on the contrary, they benefit many tropical blackwater
              fish (discus, tetras, corydoras). If you want clearer water
              without the tint, add activated carbon to the filter.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Uticaj na vodu -->
        <div class="guide-section reveal">
          <h2>Effect on water chemistry</h2>
          <p>
            Red Moor Wood naturally releases tannins — organic acids that
            slightly lower pH and reduce water hardness. This can be beneficial
            or neutral, depending on the fish species you keep:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Beneficial for:</strong> tropical blackwater fish — discus,
              angelfish, neon tetras, apistogrammas and most corydoras. These
              fish naturally live in tannin-rich water.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Neutral for:</strong> most ornamental fish that tolerate a
              wider pH range (6.5–7.5).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Monitor parameters:</strong> if you keep fish that need a
              high pH (African lake cichlids, goldfish), test your water
              regularly and use a buffer if needed.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            How strong the effect is depends on the size of the wood, the
            aquarium volume and the amount of water passing through the filter.
            In most cases the effect is minimal after the initial soaking
            period.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Odrzavanje -->
        <div class="guide-section reveal">
          <h2>Maintenance</h2>
          <p>
            Red Moor Wood needs minimal maintenance. During regular aquarium
            cleaning, gently brush the surface of the wood to remove algae
            buildup or sediment. Do not use chemical cleaning products.
          </p>
          <p>
            Over time a thin layer of biofilm will form on the wood — a white
            or grayish coating that is completely normal and beneficial. It is
            a food source for shrimp and young fish. If you don't like how it
            looks, gently brush it off during a regular water change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Velicine i dostupnost -->
        <div class="guide-section reveal">
          <h2>Sizes and availability</h2>
          <p>
            We stock Red Moor Wood roots in various sizes — from smaller pieces
            suitable for nano aquariums (under 30 liters) to large, lavish
            pieces for setups of 100 liters and more. Every piece is unique in
            shape, branching and texture.
          </p>
          <p>
            For current availability and prices, contact us:
          </p>
          <div class="guide-cta-links">
            <a href="tel:+381693301474" class="btn btn-primary" onclick="gtag('event','telefon_click',{'metod':'poziv'})">Call us</a>
            <a href="https://wa.me/381693301474?text=I%27m%20interested%20in%20a%20Red%20Moor%20Wood%20root%20for%20my%20aquarium" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-outline" onclick="gtag('event','kontakt_click',{'metod':'whatsapp'})">WhatsApp inquiry</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Interni linkovi -->
        <div class="guide-section reveal">
          <h2>More about aquariums and equipment</h2>
          <p>
            Browse our full range of aquariums, decorations and equipment:
          </p>
          <div class="usl-more-grid" style="margin-top: 20px">
            <a class="usl-link" href="/en/aquariums.html">Aquariums and decorations<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"></path></svg></a>
            <a class="usl-link" href="/en/equipment.html">Terrarium and aquarium equipment<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"></path></svg></a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>
<section class="zcta">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Contact us</h2>
        <p>
          Call us or message us on WhatsApp and we will be happy to help you
